      <div class="bg-surface-soft p-5 rounded-xl border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-ink">1. Forward Osmosis Spiegler-Kedem Solute Rejection & Van 't Hoff Potential</h4>
        <p>
          Forward osmosis operates via spontaneous chemical potential gradients across an asymmetric semi-permeable membrane without requiring high hydraulic drive pressures. The pure water flux ($J_w$) across the active polyamide layer is governed by the osmotic pressure differential:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface rounded-lg font-mono text-[11px] text-emerald-300 border border-hairline/50">
          J_w = A · [ σ · (π_draw - π_feed) - ΔP ]<br/>
          J_s = B · (C_draw - C_feed)
        </div>
        <p>
          where A is the pure water permeability coefficient, B is the solute permeability coefficient, σ is the reflection coefficient (≈ 1.0), and π is the osmotic pressure calculated via the extended Van 't Hoff relationship. Because ΔP ≈ 0, fouling cake compaction and irreversible pore plugging are suppressed by over 90% compared to reverse osmosis (RO).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-surface-soft p-5 rounded-xl border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-ink">2. Mullins-Sekerka Planar Ice Front Stability & Cryogenic Solute Exclusion</h4>
        <p>
          In Stage 2 Progressive Freeze Concentration (PFC), pre-concentrated liquid passes through tubular cooling jackets where ice crystals grow inward as a solid annular cylindrical layer. Solute molecules (sugars, proteins, polyphenols) are rejected from the advancing solid-liquid phase boundary into the bulk liquid, governed by the effective partition coefficient:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-surface rounded-lg font-mono text-[11px] text-emerald-300 border border-hairline/50">
          K_eff = C_ice / C_liquid = K₀ / [ K₀ + (1 - K₀) · exp(-v_ice · δ / D) ]<br/>
          v_crit = D · G_L / (m_L · C_L · (1 - K₀)/K₀)
        </div>
        <p>
          Maintaining the linear ice growth velocity v_ice &le; 2.5 mm/h keeps the Mullins-Sekerka planar interface morphologically stable. This prevents dendritic finger trapping, ensuring the harvested ice is chemically pure potable water with less than 0.15% solute loss.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
